    stem: '<p>Explain how bright light therapy could be used to treat an older person with Advanced Sleep Phase Disorder.</p>',
    hint: 'When should the light be given, and how does it shift the cycle?',
    criteria: ['Bright artificial light exposure in the evening (before their usual early bedtime)', 'Light detected by the retina signals the SCN to inhibit the pineal gland\'s melatonin release, delaying sleepiness', 'Over time this shifts (delays) their circadian rhythm later, so they fall asleep and wake later'],
    model: '<p>The person would sit in front of a bright light box in the evening, before their usual early bedtime. Light detected by the retina signals the suprachiasmatic nucleus, which inhibits melatonin release from the pineal gland, so they don\'t feel sleepy as early. With repeated evening exposure, their circadian rhythm is shifted later, so they fall asleep and wake at more typical times.</p>',
